// Galdrastafir: the Icelandic Staves — drawn from the manuscript leaves.
//
// From the wing's research file galdrastafir.verified.json, with its
// fact-check corrections applied: the Huld manuscript is ÍB 383 4to, not
// Lbs 143 8vo (a different book, the Galdrakver with the oldest eight-armed
// ægishjálmur); the first witness is AM 434 a 12mo, not the Galdrabók. This
// entry is not an alphabet. Its units are seven named staves, none keyed to a
// sound, so every value is null and the aim the manuscripts give each stave
// stands in its valueNote, which the register prints under the name.
//
// Five staves are drawn, each traced from one manuscript leaf in the
// handrit.is images (traced/galdrastafir/glyphs.json, independently checked):
// each glyph is the traced path data unchanged, with the stave's own measured
// pen (pen, the plaque) and the heaviest pen that keeps it legible at text
// size (textPen). The butter-knot and the helm of concealment keep glyph: null,
// for no leaf bearing their names was found. The tracing settled the leaves
// of the ægishjálmur (fol. 11r) and of the vegvísir (p. 60, fol. 26v), that
// the Huld vegvísir has no ring, and the leaf of the wrestling pair
// (Lbs 2413 8vo, fol. 20v, where the first is named Gapandi). No photograph is
// shown: the library's records restrict their use.
//
// Points the fact-check left unresolved are given with both readings and
// their sources, never as settled: the Huld date (1847 or 1860); the
// foliation of the vegvísir's leaf in Huld; the date of Lbs 143 8vo (1670 or
// the mid-17th century); the date, hands and repository
// of AM 434 a 12mo; the volume and page of Jón Árnason's ægishjálmur passage;
// the day of the 1656 burning; the number burned (21 or 22); the date of
// Flowers's second edition; the count of spells in Lbs 2413 8vo; the Galdrabók's
// shelfmark and provenance; the manuscript sources of the butter-knot and the
// helm of concealment.
// Left out: the folklore periodical Huld, Flowers's spell numbering, the
// stave against anger drawn with yarrow and blood, the titles of Pagani's
// article and of Ólafur Davíðsson's 1903 article, the date of Angurgapi, and
// every description of a stave's form.
