    status: 'ready',
    summary: 'Galdrastafir, “staves”, are magical figures, most of them named, which Icelandic manuscripts say are to be carved, drawn or carried for a stated purpose: protection, victory, finding the way, winning at wrestling, catching thieves. The oldest known Icelandic manuscript with drawn staves, the medical book AM 434 a 12mo, is of about 1500 or the late fifteenth century; the oldest substantial collection, the Galdrabók, is put in the sixteenth century by its first editor and between the late sixteenth and the mid-seventeenth by later accounts. The eight-armed ægishjálmur first appears in the seventeenth century and the vegvísir in the nineteenth. No stave of this kind is attested from the Viking Age.',
    intro: [
        'A galdrastafur, a “magic stave”, is a drawn figure with a task, and usually a name. The Icelandic books of magic that survive from about 1500 to the nineteenth century give each stave a purpose — to turn aside an enemy’s anger, to win at wrestling, to keep a traveller from losing the way in a snowstorm, to make a thief give back what was taken — and an instruction: carve it, draw it, cast or press it in lead, carry it on the body or put it in a shoe, often with words to be spoken. The staves are not letters. They stand for no sound; some include runes or Latin letters, set among strokes that belong to no alphabet.',
        'Two of them, the eight-armed ægishjálmur and the vegvísir, are now familiar far beyond Iceland, on jewellery, souvenirs and tattoos, and are often called “Viking”. The manuscripts tell a later story. The oldest known Icelandic book with drawn staves, the medical book AM 434 a 12mo, is of about 1500, or a little earlier by some accounts. The eight-armed ægishjálmur is first found in a seventeenth-century book of charms, Lbs 143 8vo, and the vegvísir only in the nineteenth century, in a compilation made by Geir Vigfússon of Akureyri, the Huld manuscript. No stave of this kind is known from the Viking Age.',
        'What is old is a name. In the Edda the ægishjálmr, the “helm of terror”, is something the dragon Fáfnir wears over the sons of men as he lies on his treasure; nothing is drawn there, and the early modern stave borrows the name. The charms around the staves are as mixed as their strokes: the spoken formulas of the Galdrabók, the oldest substantial collection, call on Christ beside Óðinn, Þórr and Freyja, and on figures from demonology. Where the staves themselves came from is argued — from bind-runes, some writers hold; from the learned magic of the Continent and the Mediterranean, others — and is not settled.',
        'In their own time the staves were also evidence at law. In the Icelandic witch trials of 1625 to 1683, owning staves, papers of runes or a book of black art could be held against the accused, and twenty-one or twenty-two people, by different counts, were burned, all but one of them men. This page gives the staves with their stated purposes and, where it is known, the book each comes from. It shows each figure as traced from one named manuscript leaf, because one name can cover several different figures, and much of what now circulates as galdrastafir has been redrawn, tidied or newly made; two staves for which no named leaf was found are left undrawn.',
    ],
