        follows: 'One manuscript leaf for each stave, in the page images of Landsbókasafn Íslands – Háskólabókasafn, Reykjavík, served by handrit.is: the Ægishjálmur from Lbs 143 8vo, Galdrakver (1670 in the handrit.is record; about the mid-17th century in Vísindavefurinn), fol. 11r; the Vegvísir from the Huld manuscript, ÍB 383 4to (1847 or 1860), p. 60 (fol. 26v in the handrit.is foliation), No. XXVII; the Þjófastafur from the same manuscript, p. 57 (fol. 25r), No. XVII; and Gapaldur and Ginfaxi from Lbs 2413 8vo, Rúna- og galdrakver (1780–1820 in the handrit.is listing), fol. 20v. No printed edition is used. The photographs are not reproduced here: the library’s records mark them for scholarly use.',
        drawn: 'Each stave is traced as the centre line of the scribe’s strokes from the earliest copy seen that bears its name, one figure to a box. The drawing keeps what the leaf shows: every arm and ending, the crossbars and forks, which ends are open rings and which are dots, the hairlines that join heavier strokes, and the parts that are faint. The names and captions written in and around the figures are not drawn, nor are specks and blots that are not part of a figure; each note says which. The rest is the site’s convention: an even round pen in place of the scribe’s swelling and thinning line, and a lighter pen than the other alphabets of the wing, because the figures are drawn in fine lines that the house pen would fill into blots — in the plaque, each stave’s own measured weight (1 to 1.5 units of the 100-unit box), and at text size the heaviest pen at which its crossbars and forks stay apart (1.5 to 2.5). Two staves are not drawn, because no leaf bearing their names was found.',
        variants: 'One name does not mean one shape, and the later copies differ. The eight-armed helm of Lbs 2917 a 4to (1868–69, p. 72) meets at a point with no central ring, the copy nearest the modern standard helm; the nine “Ægirs Hjálmar” of Lbs 2413 8vo (fol. 31v) are none of them the helm of Lbs 143 8vo; and Huld’s No. XXI, “Salomons Insigli”, is an eight-armed figure of the same family under another name. Vísindavefurinn treats a four-armed, unnamed stave against anger in AM 434 a 12mo as an older form of the helm, and that book already has unnamed staves with eight arms. Lbs 2917 a 4to (p. 27) keeps the Vegvísir’s scheme but changes several endings, and Lbs 4627 8vo (fol. 17v) changes more. No second copy of Gapandi or of Huld’s No. XVII was found, and Lbs 2917 a 4to gives the name Ginfaxi to other figures. The modern “standard” shapes on jewellery, souvenirs and websites are selections, often redrawn and tidied, and none is used as a base.',
