    links: [
        { to: 'runes', relation: 'Overlap and contrast. Some staves include runes or rune-like strokes, and the Icelandic books of magic often copy rune rows and cipher alphabets as well; the Huld manuscript holds about 300 alphabets. The staves themselves are not runes, have no sound values and are not of the Viking Age.' },
        { to: 'charakteres', relation: 'Like the charaktêres, the staves are signs that stand for no sound, drawn for a named magical aim. Pagani (Gripla, 2025) argues that they descend from Mediterranean esoteric motifs; that is argued, not shown stave by stave.' },
        { to: 'planetary-seals', relation: 'A continental parallel: Solomonic and planetary seals and characters circulated in the Danish and Norwegian “black books” (svartebøker) and their sources, and Vlasatý links the vegvísir to the “Solomon” tradition. Their influence on the Icelandic staves is argued, not demonstrated stave by stave.' },
        { to: 'hebrew', relation: 'why.is says the spells of AM 434 a 12mo, the oldest Icelandic manuscript with drawn staves, mix Christian prayers with Hebrew divine names.' },
        { to: 'latin', relation: 'Some staves are built partly from Latin letters, as others are from runes, and the Icelandic charm books set Latin, garbled Latin and divine-name formulas beside Icelandic ones: a sign of contact with the learned charms of the Continent.' },
    ],
    cautions: [
        'Five staves are drawn, each from one named manuscript leaf; the butter-knot and the helm of concealment are not, because no leaf bearing their names was found. A drawing presented as a manuscript’s that was not taken from it would be worse than a gap.',
        'The Huld manuscript is ÍB 383 4to, of the 19th century. Lbs 143 8vo is a different book: the Galdrakver of 1670, or about the mid-17th century, with the oldest eight-armed ægishjálmur.',
        'Several dates and places are disputed, and both readings are given: the Huld manuscript, 1847 or 1860; the Galdrabók, “the 1500s” (Lindqvist’s title) or about 1600 into the mid-17th century; AM 434 a 12mo, the late 15th century or about 1500; the 1656 burning, 10 April or the first day of summer; the vegvísir’s leaf in Huld, fol. 26v in the handrit.is foliation or 27r in other accounts.',
        'No stave is of the Viking Age, and none is “ancient”. “Viking compass” and similar labels are modern.',
        'Name traps: Geir Vigfússon (1813–1880), who compiled the Huld manuscript, is not Guðbrandur Vigfússon (1827–1889), the lexicographer of Cleasby–Vigfússon; and the ægis- of ægishjálmur is not the sea-giant Ægir in the dictionaries checked.',
        'One name does not mean one shape. Ægishjálmur, þjófastafur and hulinhjálmur are each given to several different figures, and the vegvísir differs between manuscripts, so every stave shown carries the manuscript leaf it was traced from.',
        'Much of what circulates as galdrastafir, on jewellery, souvenirs and websites, is redrawn, simplified or newly invented, and Justin Foster notes that the Huld material in Flowers comes at several removes from the manuscript. A stave is accepted here only with a manuscript shelfmark or a scholarly edition behind it.',
        'Kålund’s, Lindqvist’s and Flowers’s editions were not read for this page; what is said of them rests on the accounts named beside each point. The staves drawn are traced from the library’s own page images on handrit.is, and the leaves, dates and sizes given for them follow its records.',
        'Stephen E. Flowers, whose Galdrabók is the standard English translation, is a runologist by training and, as Edred Thorsson, a founder of a living esoteric order, the Rune-Gild. His editions are cited here for their texts; the emblems and texts of living organisations, among them the modern heathen bodies that use the staves, are not reproduced.',
        'The witch trials concern real people who were put to death. They are reported here from the records, and the staves are presented neither as effective nor as fraudulent.',
    ],
